1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,230
Heads, I riffle shuffle a sorted deck four times.

2
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:06,460
Tails, a computer shuffles it perfectly.

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:09,820
From the deck alone, how often could you tell which?

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:13,580
Pick fifty, seventy five, or a hundred percent.

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,660
Monte Carlo samplers move by random steps, like these riffles.

6
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:23,070
Stopped too early, they still remember where they started.

7
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:30,006
The obvious check is the top card: here's how often each card lands there after four riffles.

8
00:00:30,366 --> 00:00:34,226
Only the old top card stands out: one time in fifteen.

9
00:00:34,726 --> 00:00:36,326
Judge by it, and you're right

10
00:00:36,626 --> 00:00:38,626
fifty two percent of the time.

11
00:00:39,526 --> 00:00:41,486
Four riffles look like plenty.

12
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:47,556
The model cuts near the middle, then drops cards from each half in proportion to its size.

13
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:54,953
Each half stays in order, so one riffle leaves two rising sequences: runs of consecutive cards.

14
00:00:55,333 --> 00:00:57,253
Give each sequence its own level.

15
00:00:57,553 --> 00:01:02,693
The cut crosses each level once, so a riffle can at most split each level in two.

16
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,290
Three riffles,

17
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:05,740
eight.

18
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:07,410
Four riffles,

19
00:01:08,010 --> 00:01:10,800
at most sixteen; this deck has fourteen.

20
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:15,783
A perfect shuffle gives sixteen or fewer once in two million decks.

21
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:21,316
Call sixteen or fewer riffled, and you'd be wrong once in four million games.

22
00:01:22,216 --> 00:01:26,136
The top card can't see this: it lives in the order between cards.

23
00:01:26,833 --> 00:01:32,723
Bayer and Diaconis proved that in this model, decks with the same count are equally likely.

24
00:01:33,766 --> 00:01:35,686
So pile decks onto their counts,

25
00:01:36,286 --> 00:01:39,466
then every possible deck, weighted by its chance.

26
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:43,903
The red area, purple above blue, is the distance from random.

27
00:01:44,566 --> 00:01:47,526
How many more riffles until it's below a half?

28
00:01:49,233 --> 00:01:51,433
A fifth riffle: point nine two.

29
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:53,763
A sixth: point six one.

30
00:01:54,263 --> 00:01:56,083
A seventh: point three three.

31
00:01:57,366 --> 00:02:02,906
Guess riffled where purple is higher: you catch sixty five percent of riffled decks.

32
00:02:03,206 --> 00:02:06,456
You also flag thirty two percent of perfect ones.

33
00:02:06,756 --> 00:02:11,796
Accuracy is a half, plus half the difference, and the difference is the red area.

34
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:18,853
Each riffle roughly halves the gap; blue's spread, its standard deviation, stays about two.

35
00:02:19,353 --> 00:02:23,233
While the gap spans several spreads, the distance stays near one.

36
00:02:23,733 --> 00:02:27,353
Within one spread, it halves every riffle: that's the cliff.

37
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:33,083
Multiply each gap by two to the k, and it settles near two hundred thirty.

38
00:02:34,683 --> 00:02:38,853
Set the gap equal to one spread, and k is just under seven.

39
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:49,096
For n cards, two hundred thirty grows like n squared, but the spread like root n, so the cliff sits near three halves log base two of n.

40
00:02:49,766 --> 00:02:51,276
Aldous proved that law.

41
00:02:51,676 --> 00:02:59,786
Bayer and Diaconis computed the exact curve: for fifty two cards, seven riffles is the first below a half.

42
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:09,196
Trefethen and Trefethen measured information instead: no cliff; five or six shuffles do a reasonable job.

43
00:03:09,866 --> 00:03:19,256
Many Markov chains share this cliff, and samplers share the trap: one statistic, like the top card, settles long before the whole chain.

44
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:21,353
Back to the coin flip.

45
00:03:21,653 --> 00:03:22,963
Three more riffles,

46
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:26,633
and you'd be right only two times out of three.

47
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:30,076
Four riffles fool someone watching the top card.

48
00:03:30,376 --> 00:03:33,126
Fooling someone who counts sequences takes ten.

